17" "q-37085/selection/c2b4e34afc2c763a79d25da189720d891903dc9d39163bb5c2e6c05f71743655"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37085" :question/id #uuid "1c3b0ba8-8f1e-5b85-96e6-708a54bce1fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-37085" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-2)I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37085/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37085"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
